receptors.

6
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:17,540
In a conscious action, a stimulus is detected by a receptor.

7
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:22,100
Sensory neurons carry the impulse to the spinal cord and brain.

8
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:26,180
The brain decides on a response and motor neurons carry

9
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:27,700
the impulse to an effector.

10
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:31,400
In many spinal reflexes, the immediate response is

11
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:35,500
coordinated through the spinal cord without waiting for a conscious decision.

12
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:40,020
Sensory neurons send the impulse to a relay neuron in the spinal

13
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:42,460
cord which sends it to a motor neuron.

14
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:44,140
This is much faster.

15
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:46,160
Neurons don't touch its synapses.

16
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:49,380
Chemicals called neurotransmitters cross the gap.

17
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:52,540
Eyes focus light using the lens and cornea.

18
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:58,160
A reflex arc begins when a reflex arc begins when a receptor

19
00:00:58,160 --> 00:00:59,160
detects a stimulus.

20
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:03,240
An impulse travels along a sensory neuron to the central nervous

21
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:07,160
system where it may pass through a relay neuron before traveling

22
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:09,340
along a motor neuron to an effector.

23
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:13,600
Reflexes are automatic and can be rapid because they do not require

24
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,640
a conscious decision before the response.

25
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:18,860
The brain can still receive information about the event.

26
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:23,040
At a chemical synapse, neurotransmitter molecules diffuse

27
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:26,120
across the synaptic cleft and bind to receptors.

